    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1402-a1">

                <pb facs="1106" n="990" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg990"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Consul to the
                            American Minister</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Fez</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1912-05-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 23, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my letter of the 16th
                    instant, I have the honour to report that on the 20th instant the Kasbah Filala
                    people imprisoned in connection with the incident of the 18th April were
                    released.</p>
                <p>As no letter or further information had reached me about Hadj Mommed Lehsen, I
                    enquired yesterday at the French Consulate how his case stood. M. Mercier, the
                    Vice Consul, explained that the Reporter of the Court Martial had power to
                    release accused persons when he thought no case was likely to be brought against
                    them, but in any event the information lodged, and the Reporter’s proceedings
                    had to be submitted to the Court Martial. The latter then formally dismissed the
                    case—or re-summoned the parties—as the Court might think fit. Such proceedings
                    were usually (in cases like the present) merely formal, but still M. Mercier
                    thought the most prudent course would be for Hadj Mohammed Lehsen to remain at
                    this Consulate until the Court Martial had formally dismissed the complaint.
                    Under the circumstances it was unlikely the Court would make any written
                    communication to anybody, and so M. Mercier said that—in a personal way—he would
                    arrange to learn when the Court Martial had formally disposed of the case and
                    would inform me thereof, so that Hadj Mohammed Lehsen might then leave the
                    Consulate without possibility of molestation in this affair.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. M. Macleod.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
